1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,734
So, what is konspekt really?

2
00:00:02,804 --> 00:00:12,639
Technically, it's just a directory that lives inside your repository — one place that holds your project's evolving state: the decisions, the tasks, the context behind them.

3
00:00:12,709 --> 00:00:16,208
Think of it as the memory your project keeps about itself.

4
00:00:16,978 --> 00:00:18,728
And it travels with you.

5
00:00:18,798 --> 00:00:25,689
Use it inside your IDE, in a local Gen-AI app, or on the web — anywhere, as long as there's a repo behind it.

6
00:00:25,759 --> 00:00:27,403
No repo yet?

7
00:00:27,473 --> 00:00:32,060
No problem: seed a session with the konspekt web-and-mobile prompt and you're off.

8
00:00:32,830 --> 00:00:42,409
The first time you actually notice konspekt is right at the start of a session, when it asks you one simple thing: which node in the graph do you want to bind this conversation to?

9
00:00:43,179 --> 00:00:45,079
Not sure what to bind to?

10
00:00:45,149 --> 00:00:49,437
Pop open the IntelliJ plugin and pick the node you want to work on.

11
00:00:49,507 --> 00:00:51,812
Working outside IntelliJ?

12
00:00:51,882 --> 00:00:56,875
There's a standalone UI app that ships with the tool and does exactly the same job.

13
00:00:57,645 --> 00:01:00,099
That UI is also where you browse.

14
00:01:00,169 --> 00:01:07,295
Filter and search across nodes in every state — it's basically your to-do list and a lightweight project manager in one view.

15
00:01:08,065 --> 00:01:11,138
From there, konspekt quietly keeps the receipts.

16
00:01:11,207 --> 00:01:13,747
It tracks which commands were executed.

17
00:01:14,067 --> 00:01:16,009
which files were changed.

18
00:01:16,329 --> 00:01:19,124
and, maybe most importantly, who said what

19
00:01:19,894 --> 00:01:29,922
Every provenance record is backed by a hashed, verbatim transcript of the decision-making chain — so the trail isn't a tidy summary after the fact, it's the real conversation.

20
00:01:30,692 --> 00:01:37,434
And that pays off the moment you need to trace your ASR and ADR roots and ask, "wait, why did we decide this?"

21
00:01:38,204 --> 00:01:42,791
Here's the best part: you don't have to do anything special to make any of this happen.

22
00:01:42,861 --> 00:01:49,411
A konspekt-equipped agent suggests the state changes for you — creating nodes, accepting them, moving them along.

23
00:01:50,181 --> 00:01:52,336
Here's a task being proposed.

24
00:01:52,656 --> 00:01:55,473
and here it is, completed and accepted

25
00:01:56,243 --> 00:02:03,476
Finally, a couple of git checks keep everyone honest — for example, you have to accept a task before you're allowed to commit against it.

26
00:02:04,246 --> 00:02:05,953
So that's konspekt.

27
00:02:06,023 --> 00:02:09,586
The agent proposes, you accept, and git keeps the record.

28
00:02:09,656 --> 00:02:17,252
It's open source under FINOS Labs, so grab it from the repo on screen, try it on a project of your own, and tell us what you think.

